diff --git a/flatpak/README.md b/flatpak/README.md
index a2a6d11ac3700a85e7d944d4e4d551d6ea732900..d0fd0719a24f097e0fe205474c1538cafae62f72 100644
--- a/flatpak/README.md
+++ b/flatpak/README.md
@@ -18,25 +18,69 @@     flatpak install --user flatpak/phial.flatpak
 
 ## Modules
 
-The manifest builds every runtime dependency from source. The list follows.
+The manifest builds only the tools that the sandbox executes. The list follows.
 
-- libtpms and swtpm supply the TPM 2.0 device.
-- socat carries the vsock forwards.
-- passt supplies the user space network.
-- Caddy injects secrets into outbound requests.
-- virtiofsd supplies the host shares.
-- QEMU runs the machine.
-- edk2 supplies the OVMF Secure Boot pair.
-- waypipe renders machine windows on the host.
-- FreeRDP supplies the RDP client.
+- waypipe renders the windows of a guest application on the desktop of the host.
+- ovmf supplies the UEFI pair. The source is the Fedora edk2-ovmf RPM, which the
+  build unpacks with bsdtar. extra-data cannot carry this pair, because it
+  fetches single files at install time and cannot unpack an RPM.
 - Phial itself is the last module.
 
+waypipe tracks v0.11.2. The build turns off the optional lz4, zstd, gbm, and
+video wrappers, because their bindgen step needs libclang, which the SDK does
+not carry. No dependency of this app is in the Flathub shared-modules
+repository.
+
+The machine tools stay on the host. The units that the host install writes run
+swtpm, virtiofsd, passt, socat, and QEMU on the host, and QEMU reads the OVMF
+pair from the host. The RDP client also runs on the host: the window starts
+xfreerdp through flatpak-spawn. A bundle copy of these tools cannot serve the
+host QEMU or the host client, because /app exists only inside the sandbox.
+
+## Permissions
+
+The manifest asks for the following and nothing more:
+
+- `--socket=wayland`, `--socket=fallback-x11`, `--share=ipc`, `--device=dri`
+  draw the window.
+- `--share=network` downloads the machine image, reaches the upstreams of
+  proxied secrets, and lists the local packages.
+- `--talk-name=org.freedesktop.secrets` stores and reads the secrets in the
+  keyring of the session.
+- `--talk-name=org.freedesktop.systemd1` starts and stops the user units that
+  run the machine, the gate, and the tools.
+- `--talk-name=org.freedesktop.Flatpak` runs the tools of the host through
+  flatpak-spawn, because ssh-add, gpg, run0, xfreerdp, and pinentry live outside
+  the sandbox.
+- `--filesystem=~/.config/phial`, `--filesystem=~/.local/share/phial`, and
+  `--filesystem=xdg-run/phial` share the state of the host install.
+- `--filesystem=~/.ssh` reads the public keys that the session agent holds.
+
+Notifications, the background request, and the file dialogs go through the
+portals, so the manifest grants no direct access to the notification service.
+The root setup runs run0 on the host through flatpak-spawn, so the sandbox
+needs no PolicyKit talk.
+
+## Host requirements
+
+The host install writes user units that run the host copies of the machine
+tools. The host therefore needs:
+
+- qemu-system-x86_64 and edk2-ovmf for the machine.
+- swtpm, virtiofsd, passt, and socat for the units.
+- ssh-agent from openssh, for the session agent.
+- ssh-add, ssh-keygen, ssh, gpg, and xfreerdp for the tools that flatpak-spawn
+  runs.
+- pinentry-gnome3 and run0 for the gate and the root setup.
+- virt-fw-vars for the Secure Boot enrollment.
+
 ## Notes
 
 - The manifest pins every archive with a sha256 sum. Run `make flatpak-lint` to
   validate the JSON alone.
 - The build needs the golang and rust-stable SDK extensions.
-- The edk2 build needs network access for its submodules.
-- A full build takes about one hour on twelve cores. QEMU and edk2 dominate.
+- flatpak-builder keeps the downloads, the git mirrors, and the module checksums
+  in .flatpak-builder, so a rerun skips the modules that already finished.
+  Deleting flatpak/build does not lose them.
 - The application module compiles the Blueprint files, the settings schema, and the
   resource bundle before it builds the binary.
